sp_widget.client_script:
function CaseLookupWidgetController($scope, $http) { /* * Case Lookup Widget - client-side AngularJS controller. * * Implements the case-status-lookup user interaction described in * AAP Section 0.4.4 ("Page 2 - Case Status Lookup: a single input * (case number) and a result panel that shows status, subject, * opened_date when found, or the literal text 'No case found with * that number.' when not found."). * * Exposed properties (template-bound): * c.caseNumber - string; bound to the input field via ng-model. * Initialized to '' (empty string). * c.result - object | null; on successful lookup, contains * exactly { status, subject, opened_date } - the * three whitelisted fields. null when no successful * lookup has occurred. Drives the result panel's * ng-if="c.result". * c.notFound - boolean; true when the case table was queried and * held no case with that number - an HTTP 404, any * other 4xx, or a 200 whose body carried no status. * Drives the not-found panel's ng-if="c.notFound", * which renders the AAP Section 0.7.4 verbatim text. * c.errorMessage - string; non-empty only when the lookup could not * be ANSWERED: a 5xx, a network failure, or the * client-side timeout expiring. Drives the separate * service-failure panel. Mutually exclusive with both * c.result and c.notFound. * c.loading - boolean; true while the in-flight $http.get * request is pending. Drives the button's * ng-disabled and the "Looking up..." text. It is * the ONLY thing that disables the button. * c.validationMessage * - string; non-empty when Look Up Status was pressed * with an empty field. Mutually exclusive with * c.result, c.notFound and c.errorMessage: every * path that sets one of the four clears the other * three. * c.pressed - boolean; true once Look Up Status has been pressed * at least once. Gates the field's own message, its * has-error treatment and its aria-invalid binding. * Replaced the control's $touched flag, whose blur * trigger moved the button between mousedown and * mouseup and so swallowed the first press. See the * flag's own comment for the measurement. * * Exposed methods (template-bound): * c.lookup(form) * - executes the case-status lookup. Sets c.pressed as * its first statement. Returns without issuing any * request when the field is empty, setting * c.validationMessage. Otherwise issues * GET /api/x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup?number=<encoded> * via $http.get() under a LOOKUP_TIMEOUT_MS deadline. * On success (HTTP 200 with a status), sets c.result * with the strict 3-field whitelist. On a 4xx, sets * c.notFound = true so the verbatim not-found message * is rendered. On a 5xx, a network failure or a * timeout, sets c.errorMessage instead, because in * those cases nothing was learned about the case. * c.onInputChange() * - bound to ng-change on the input; drops the * previous lookup's outcome - the result panel, the * not-found alert and the service-failure alert - so * a panel about a superseded case number cannot * coexist with the number now in the field. The * refusal summary is dropped only once a press would * no longer be refused; see c.clientProblemRemains(). * c.clientInvalid(control) * - the whole of the per-field invalid condition: * $invalid AND the button has been pressed. Drives * the field's message, its has-error class and its * aria-invalid binding from one expression, so the * three can never disagree. * c.clientProblemRemains() * - true while the model still holds something this * widget would refuse to look up (an empty or * whitespace-only case number). Keeps the refusal * summary on screen across an edit that leaves the * field still unusable. * c.markAllTouched(form) * - marks the registered control $touched so * AngularJS's own ng-touched state matches the * press. No longer what reveals the field's message * (c.pressed is), but kept so the framework's view * of the form stays consistent with what the page * is showing. * * One-time initialization (not template-bound): when the page was * reached with a ?number= parameter - which is how the submission * confirmation links here - the input is pre-filled from * c.data.prefilledNumber and c.lookup() is run once. See the block at * the end of this controller. * * Design rationale: * - controller-as syntax (`var c = this`) per AAP Section 0.5.1's * widget-authoring conventions and per the sibling confirmation * widget at ./sp_widget_x_casemgmt_case_confirmation_widget.xml. * - $http (NOT fetch): Service Portal widgets run in AngularJS * 1.5.x context; $http is the canonical HTTP client. fetch() * would bypass AngularJS's digest cycle and require manual * $scope.$apply() handling. * - encodeURIComponent on the case number: defensive URL escaping. * Even though normal case numbers (CASE0000001) contain no * special characters, defensive encoding prevents URL-injection * attacks if a malicious user submits a crafted string. * - Strict 3-field whitelist on response.data: defense-in-depth. * Even if the server's response somehow contained additional * fields (e.g., due to a future refactor of the Script Include), * this client controller extracts ONLY status/subject/opened_date * so no internal data leaks to the template. * - Verbatim error message in template, NOT echoed from server: * the message "No case found with that number." (AAP Section * 0.7.4) is hardcoded in the template via ng-if="c.notFound". * This controller deliberately does NOT pass error.data.error * or error.statusText to the template - the server's error * body is NEVER trusted by the client. * - Identical handling of every 4xx: information-leakage hardening. * 404 (not-found) and 400 (bad-request) both set c.notFound = * true, so an attacker cannot distinguish "case not found" from * "invalid input" and gains no case-number enumeration oracle. * A 500, a network failure and an expired deadline set * c.errorMessage instead, because in those cases the case table * was never reached and the verbatim not-found text would be a * false statement about the requester's case. * - c.loading flag: prevents double-lookup race conditions if * the user clicks the button twice quickly, AND drives the * "Looking up..." button text for clear UX feedback. */ var c = this;
/* * Deadline for the lookup request, in milliseconds. Chosen to sit well * above any normal round trip to the scripted REST endpoint (measured in * tens of milliseconds against the verification instance) while still * failing fast enough that a requester is not left staring at a disabled * button. See the comment at the $http.get call for why a deadline is * appropriate on this read and inappropriate on the sibling widget's POST. */ var LOOKUP_TIMEOUT_MS = 20000;
// Lookup state. Initialize with safe defaults so the template's // ng-if expressions evaluate cleanly even before c.lookup() runs. c.caseNumber = ''; c.result = null; c.notFound = false; c.errorMessage = ''; c.loading = false;
/** * True once Look Up Status has been pressed at least once. * * This flag, and not the control's $touched flag, is what reveals the * field's own message. The distinction is not cosmetic - it decides * whether the FIRST press of the page's primary action does anything * at all. * * $touched flips on blur. A pointer press on the button blurs the * input the requester was in, so with the message keyed to $touched * the sequence was: mousedown -> blur -> $setTouched -> the * role="alert" paragraph is inserted ABOVE the button -> the button * moves 20px down -> mouseup lands on the <form> in the gap the * button has just vacated -> ng-submit never fires. Measured on the * rendered public page x_casemgmt_case_status, with the empty field * focused and a real pointer press aimed at the button's original * centre: click handlers 0, submits 0, GET requests to * /api/x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup 0, and no summary - while the * field's error paragraph and aria-invalid="true" were already on * screen. A second press at the button's NEW position worked, so the * requester's first press on the primary public action was silently * swallowed. Typing a character and backspacing it produced the same * state without any press at all. * * Reserving the vertical space with CSS would also close it, but AAP * Section 0.4.4 fixes this portal to the platform default theme with * "No custom CSS, no custom branding" and this record's <css> element * is and stays EMPTY, so the fix is to stop the blur from changing * the layout rather than to absorb the change. * * Keying on the press instead means blur changes nothing on screen - * validity itself is already current, because AngularJS revalidates * on input rather than on blur - so no layout moves between mousedown * and mouseup and the first press is always delivered. After that * first press the flag stays true, so the message then tracks the * model live: it clears as the field is filled and reappears if it is * emptied, which is the feedback the requester needs at the point * they are acting on it, and by then the reflow happens on a * keystroke rather than under the pointer. * * The same flag and the same reasoning are carried by the sibling * submission widget at ./sp_widget_x_casemgmt_case_submission_widget.xml * (see its c.pressed); the two widgets are deliberately consistent, * because this defect was measured on both public pages. * * The one-time prefill block at the end of this controller calls * c.lookup() and therefore sets this flag without a press. That is * correct: the requester followed a link that says "Check the status * of this case", the field is populated in that path so nothing is * revealed, and no layout can move between a mousedown and a mouseup * that have not happened yet. */ c.pressed = false;
/* * Message raised when Look Up Status is pressed with no case number. * * This exists because the button is no longer disabled on an empty * field. It used to be ng-disabled on !c.caseNumber, which made the * primary action unanswerable in exactly the state a first-time * visitor arrives in: a disabled <button> is pointer-transparent, so * a click aimed at it landed on the surrounding DIV.form-group and * produced nothing at all, and a disabled button is absent from the * tab order, so a keyboard user never reached it. * * The sentence itself is held in REFUSAL_SUMMARY below so that the * two places which raise it - the empty-input branch of c.lookup() * and the re-instatement in c.onInputChange() - cannot drift apart. */ c.validationMessage = '';
/* * The one and only spelling of the refusal summary. * * It is raised in two places, and it has to be the same string in * both: c.lookup() raises it when the button is pressed on an unusable * field, and c.onInputChange() re-raises it when an edit leaves the * field unusable after such a press. Holding it in one constant is * what makes "the summary is present whenever a press has been * refused and the field is still unusable" a single fact rather than * two strings that happen to agree today. */ var REFUSAL_SUMMARY = 'Enter a case number, then choose Look Up Status.';
/** * Discard the outcome of the previous lookup. * * Bound to ng-change on the case-number input. Without it, the * previous outcome outlived the input that produced it: the verbatim * "No case found with that number." alert stayed on screen while the * requester retyped, so the page asserted something about a number * that was no longer in the field, and it could sit alongside a fresh * validation message and a stale result panel - three messages about * three different moments at once. Any keystroke means the requester * has moved on from that answer, so the three ANSWER panels are * dropped together; leaving any one of them would let a panel * describing the old number coexist with the new one. * * THE REFUSAL SUMMARY IS NOT AN ANSWER ABOUT A NUMBER, AND IS NOT * DROPPED WITH THEM. * * c.validationMessage says "Enter a case number, then choose Look Up * Status." - a statement about the field as it stands, and the only * page-level statement that the press was received and refused. * Clearing it unconditionally withdrew that statement while the * field's own role="alert" message was still on screen beside a * still-empty field: press the button on an empty form, type one * character, backspace it, and the summary was gone while "Enter a * case number to look up." remained, which reads as though the press * had never been received. So it survives an edit that leaves the * field still unusable, and is withdrawn only once a press would be * accepted - which is exactly what c.clientProblemRemains() tests. * The sibling submission widget carries the same guard for the same * reason (see its c.onInputChange). * * THE GUARD RE-INSTATES; IT DOES NOT MERELY PRESERVE. An earlier * revision returned early when c.clientProblemRemains() held, which * preserved the summary but could never bring it back. That was not * enough, and the gap was found by running the page rather than by * reading it: press the button on an empty field (summary appears), * type "C" - the field is momentarily usable, so the summary is * correctly withdrawn - then backspace. The field is empty again and * c.clientProblemRemains() is true again, but the early return had * nothing to restore, so the summary stayed gone for good while the * field's own "Enter a case number to look up." came back beside it. * That is the original defect wearing a different sequence: the page * again showed a field-level complaint with no page-level statement * that the press had been received. A single space typed into an * empty field never reproduced it, because that path never leaves the * unusable state - which is why only a real run over the exact * type-then-backspace sequence exposed it. * * So the summary is DERIVED from the two facts that justify it - a * press has happened (c.pressed) and the field is still unusable * (c.clientProblemRemains()) - and is re-raised from REFUSAL_SUMMARY * whenever both hold. c.pressed is required: before the first press * there is nothing to restate, and raising the summary on a visitor * who has only typed and deleted a character would reintroduce * exactly the unprompted, layout-shifting message that c.pressed * exists to suppress. * * NO SNAPSHOT COMPARISON IS NEEDED HERE, and its absence is * deliberate rather than an omission. The sibling widget compares the * model against a snapshot because ng-change there can fire WITHOUT * the model moving: AngularJS trims the view value before * $setViewValue, and $commitViewValue skips its unchanged-value early * return for a control with NATIVE validators, which type="email" * has. This widget's only control is a plain type="text" input with * no native validators, so both the input directive's own * `ctrl.$viewValue !== value` test and $commitViewValue's early * return hold: a space typed into an empty field commits nothing and * never reaches this function. ng-change here therefore implies a * real change, and comparing against a snapshot would compare a value * against itself. */ c.onInputChange = function() { c.result = null; c.notFound = false; c.errorMessage = ''; if (c.clientProblemRemains()) { // Re-raise rather than return: see the note above. Gated on // c.pressed so nothing is said before the first press. if (c.pressed) { c.validationMessage = REFUSAL_SUMMARY; } return; } c.validationMessage = ''; };
/** * The per-field invalid condition: this control is invalid AND the * button has been pressed. * * One expression drives all three of the field's invalid affordances - * its role="alert" message, its Bootstrap has-error treatment and its * aria-invalid binding - so the page cannot describe the control as * invalid through one channel and valid through another. * * The second half was the control's own $touched flag until this * change. See c.pressed for why it is now the press and not the blur: * keying on blur inserted the message between mousedown and mouseup * and moved the button out from under the pointer, so the first press * of Look Up Status was swallowed. * * The control is guarded against being undefined. This form is not * under an ng-if, so caseLookupForm.caseNumber does resolve here, but * a template expression can still evaluate on a digest before the * control has registered, and answering "not invalid" is the correct * answer in that state. * * @param {Object} control - an ngModel controller off the form. * @returns {boolean} true when the control is invalid and Look Up * Status has been pressed at least once. */ c.clientInvalid = function(control) { return !!(control && control.$invalid && c.pressed); };
/** * True while the model still holds something this widget would refuse * to look up. * * That is the whole of the client-side refusal rule for this page: an * empty or whitespace-only case number, tested exactly as c.lookup() * tests it, so the two can never disagree about whether a press would * be accepted. c.onInputChange() uses it to decide whether the * refusal summary may be withdrawn. * * It reads c.caseNumber rather than the FormController because * onInputChange is an ng-change handler with no form argument to * read, and the two agree by construction: emptiness is the whole of * what `required` means on this control, and AngularJS trims text * input into the model by default (ng-trim), so a whitespace-only * entry arrives here as '' exactly as it arrives at the required * validator. * * @returns {boolean} true when a press would still be refused on the * client. */ c.clientProblemRemains = function() { return !c.caseNumber || String(c.caseNumber).trim() === ''; };
/** * Mark every registered control on a form as touched. * * The field's own role="alert" message and its red has-error * treatment are bound to $invalid && c.pressed (see c.pressed for why * the second half is the press rather than the blur it used to be), * so this walk is no longer what makes them appear. It is kept * because AngularJS's own ng-touched / ng-untouched classes and * $touched state are part of the framework's public view of the form, * and leaving a pressed form describing its control as never-visited * would be inconsistent with what the page is showing. * $setSubmitted() does not cover it - it sets $submitted on the FORM, * not $touched on the controls - so the controls are walked * explicitly. Possessing $setTouched is what distinguishes an ngModel * controller from the form's own $-prefixed bookkeeping, and it is * also the guard that keeps this from throwing on a future AngularJS * whose form controller exposes different internals. * * @param {Object} form - the AngularJS form controller. */ c.markAllTouched = function(form) { if (!form) { return; } if (typeof form.$setSubmitted === 'function') { form.$setSubmitted(); } for (var key in form) { if (key.charAt(0) === '$') { continue; } var control = form[key]; if (control && typeof control.$setTouched === 'function') { control.$setTouched(); } } };
/** * Look up the status of a case by its number via the scripted REST * endpoint at /api/x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup. * * Per AAP Section 0.4.4: returns ONLY status, subject, opened_date * via strict 3-field whitelist on the response. * * Per AAP Section 0.7.4: on no-match (HTTP 404 or any other error), * the verbatim text "No case found with that number." is rendered * by the template. This function MUST NOT echo error.data.error or * any server-supplied text. * * @param {Object} [submittedForm] - the caseLookupForm * FormController, passed by the template's ng-submit. This form is * NOT under an ng-if, so $scope.caseLookupForm does resolve here * and the fallback below is genuinely a fallback - but the * controller is passed explicitly anyway, so the method does not * depend on where in the scope hierarchy the form happens to * link. The sibling submission widget's form IS under an ng-if, * and reading it off $scope there silently skipped validation * altogether; taking the argument removes that failure mode from * both widgets rather than from one. */ c.lookup = function(submittedForm) { // Record that a press has been delivered. This is the flag the // field's own message, its has-error treatment and its // aria-invalid binding are keyed to (see c.pressed), and it is set // here - FIRST statement, before any branch - so that a press // answered by the empty-field guard below and a press that reaches // the endpoint both reveal what explains them, and so that nothing // reveals them BEFORE a press, which is what kept the button from // moving out from under the pointer. c.pressed = true; // Idempotent guard: prevent double-lookup if the user clicks // the button or presses Enter while a request is in flight. if (c.loading) { return; } // Empty-input guard, and the ANSWER to a press on an empty form. // // The button is no longer disabled while the field is empty - a // disabled button is pointer-transparent and out of the tab order, // so the press went unanswered (see c.validationMessage) - so this // branch is what the press produces. c.pressed above is what flips // the field's own role="alert" message into the DOM; the walk below // keeps AngularJS's own $touched bookkeeping in step with it, and // this branch sets the summary beside the button. // // No request is issued: the return happens before the $http.get, // exactly as the disabled button previously ensured. if (!c.caseNumber || String(c.caseNumber).trim() === '') { c.markAllTouched(submittedForm || $scope.caseLookupForm); c.result = null; c.notFound = false; c.errorMessage = ''; c.validationMessage = REFUSAL_SUMMARY; return; }
// Reset state at the start of every lookup. The three outcome // panels are mutually exclusive: at most one of c.result, // c.notFound and c.errorMessage is truthy at any time. c.loading = true; c.result = null; c.notFound = false; c.errorMessage = ''; c.validationMessage = '';
// Build the URL with defensive URL-encoding of the case number. // The server-side Script Include x_casemgmt.CasePortalService. // lookupCase() trims whitespace and queries by the case 'number' // field. encodeURIComponent() handles any whitespace, special // characters, or potentially malicious input safely. var url = '/api/x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup?number=' + encodeURIComponent(String(c.caseNumber).trim());
// The deadline below is what stops a lookup hanging indefinitely. // Without it, an unresponsive instance leaves c.loading true for as // long as the browser is willing to wait, the button stays disabled // reading "Looking up...", and the requester has no way to retry // short of reloading the page. On expiry AngularJS rejects the // promise with status 0, which the error handler reports as a // service failure rather than as a missing case. // // A deadline is safe HERE because this request is a read: retrying it // costs nothing and cannot duplicate anything. The sibling submission // widget deliberately does NOT set one on its POST, because a // submission that succeeds server-side just after the client gives up // would tell the requester it failed and invite a second press, which // is how duplicate cases get created. $http.get(url, { timeout: LOOKUP_TIMEOUT_MS }).then(function(response) { // Success path (HTTP 200). The server's REST operation // returns { status, subject, opened_date } per its contract - // but a ServiceNow Scripted REST API wraps whatever the // operation script passes to response.setBody() inside a // `result` envelope before it reaches the wire. Measured // against the live endpoint, a 200 body is // {"result":{"status":"Draft","subject":"...","opened_date":"..."}} // so the three whitelisted values live at // response.data.result.*, and reading response.data.* yields // undefined - which would have painted a result panel with // three blank rows instead of the case's status. // // The unwrap below prefers the envelope and falls back to the // bare body, so it is correct whether or not the envelope is // applied (the platform can be configured either way, and a // caller may append sysparm_no_envelope). c.loading = false; var body = (response && response.data && response.data.result) ? response.data.result : (response ? response.data : null); // `status` is mandatory on every case, so its presence is the // signal that a real record came back. An empty or // status-less body is treated as not-found rather than // rendered as a panel of blanks. if (body && body.status) { // Strict 3-field whitelist (defense-in-depth). Extract // ONLY status, subject, opened_date from the response // body. Even if the server were misconfigured to return // additional fields, this widget would not display them. // String() coercion handles undefined/null gracefully - // the template renders an empty cell rather than the // literal text "undefined" or "null". c.result = { status: String(body.status || ''), subject: String(body.subject || ''), opened_date: String(body.opened_date || '') }; c.notFound = false; } else { // Defensive: empty or status-less response body on HTTP // 200. Treat as not-found rather than rendering an empty // result panel. // This should not occur in practice (the server always // returns a body on 200) but guards against unexpected // upstream behavior. c.result = null; c.notFound = true; } }, function(error) { // Error path. Split in two, because a lookup that was ANSWERED // and found nothing is a different fact from a lookup that was // never answered. // // 4xx -> the request reached the case table and no case with // that number came back. This is the not-found case, // and it renders the AAP Section 0.7.4 verbatim text. // EVERY 4xx maps here, not just 404, so a 400 and a // 404 remain indistinguishable to the caller and no // case-number enumeration oracle is created. // 5xx, // 0/-1 -> the instance failed, the network failed, or the // deadline above expired. Nothing was learned about // the case, so claiming "No case found with that // number." would be a false statement about the // requester's case - and a costly one, because the // requester concludes their case does not exist. This // renders the separate service-failure panel instead. // // Neither branch echoes error.data.error, error.statusText or any // other server-supplied content: both message strings are // hardcoded, in this controller and in the template respectively. c.loading = false; c.result = null; var httpStatus = (error && typeof error.status === 'number') ? error.status : 0; if (httpStatus >= 400 && httpStatus < 500) { c.notFound = true; c.errorMessage = ''; } else { c.notFound = false; c.errorMessage = 'The case service could not be reached. Please try again in a moment.'; } }); };
/* * If this page was reached from a submission confirmation, honour the * case number that link carried. * * The confirmation's anchor is labelled "Check the status of this * case" and carries ?number=<the case number>. Pre-filling alone would * still leave the requester to press a button to be told something the * link had already promised, so the lookup is run once here as well. * That is the requester driving it - they clicked a link that says * exactly this - and it is the only circumstance in which this widget * issues a request without a press: with no parameter, * c.data.prefilledNumber is '' and nothing happens. * * It runs after c.lookup is defined, and it runs ONCE - there is no * watcher, so editing the field afterwards behaves normally and a * reload with the same URL simply repeats the same single read. The * request is a read, so repeating it costs nothing and can duplicate * nothing. * * Going through c.lookup() also sets c.pressed, which is harmless and * correct here: the field is populated in this path, so nothing is * revealed by it, and the reveal it gates can only move layout on a * later keystroke - never between a mousedown and a mouseup, which is * the sequence c.pressed exists to keep clear. * * The server-script data is read from c.data OR $scope.data. The * Service Portal framework populates both - it puts `data` on the * widget's scope and aliases the controller instance as `c` - but * which of the two is already assigned at controller-CONSTRUCTION * time depends on the framework version's binding order, and this * runs at construction time rather than from an event handler. Taking * either source removes that dependency. Both being absent means the * widget was instantiated outside the framework entirely (a preview * harness, a future embed), and the guard degrades to doing nothing * rather than throwing and taking the whole widget down for the sake * of an optional convenience. */ var widgetData = c.data || $scope.data; if (widgetData && widgetData.prefilledNumber) { c.caseNumber = widgetData.prefilledNumber; c.lookup(); } }
